/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/sc-500.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("sc-500", [["az1",0,1,"An engineer needs to give a helpdesk team the ability to reset passwords for non-admin users in Microsoft Entra ID, and nothing else. Which role should be assigned?",["User Administrator","Helpdesk Administrator","Global Reader","Privileged Authentication Administrator"],1,"Helpdesk Administrator can reset passwords for non-administrators and some limited admin roles. User Administrator also manages users and groups, which is more than required.","1: Entra roles",["User Administrator can create, delete and manage all non-admin users and groups, far more than the password-reset-only scope requested, breaking least privilege.",null,"Global Reader is read-only across the tenant and cannot reset any passwords.","Privileged Authentication Administrator can reset credentials and MFA for administrators too, a much broader and more sensitive scope than non-admin resets."]],["az2",0,1,"Admins hold standing Owner rights on production subscriptions. The security team wants them to request Owner only when needed, with MFA and a written reason, for at most four hours. What should be configured?",["An Azure Policy Deny assignment on the Owner role","A Conditional Access policy for the Azure portal app","A custom role with fewer actions than Owner","A PIM eligible assignment with activation settings"],3,"PIM for Azure resources turns Owner into an eligible role that must be activated with MFA and justification for a limited duration. Conditional Access controls sign-in, not standing role membership.","1: Privileged Identity Management",["Azure Policy governs resource configuration and cannot control role membership or turn Owner into a request-on-demand assignment.","Conditional Access enforces sign-in conditions like MFA and location but does not remove the standing Owner assignment itself.","A custom role reduces permissions, but they would still be held permanently rather than activated for a limited window with justification.",null]],["az3",0,1,"Which statement about Azure RBAC role assignment inheritance is correct?",["Assignments at a management group are inherited by child subscriptions","Assignments at a resource group apply to the subscription above it","Resource-level assignments override deny assignments","Only Owner assignments are inherited downward"],0,"Azure RBAC assignments flow down the scope hierarchy: management group, subscription, resource group, resource. They never flow upward, and deny assignments take precedence over role assignments.","1: Azure RBAC",[null,"Assignments never flow upward, so a resource group assignment does not apply to its parent subscription.","Deny assignments always take precedence over role assignments, so resource-level assignments cannot override them.","All role assignments inherit downward, not only Owner."]],["az4",0,1,"A web app running in App Service must read secrets from Key Vault without any credential stored in code or configuration. What should be used?",["A client secret saved in app settings","The Key Vault access key","A managed identity for the web app","A shared access signature"],2,"A managed identity gets tokens from Microsoft Entra automatically, and the vault grants that identity a role such as Key Vault Secrets User. A client secret in app settings is still a stored credential. Key Vault has no access key or SAS.","1: Managed identities",["A client secret in app settings is still a stored credential that can leak and must be rotated, which the requirement forbids.","Key Vault has no 'access key'; access is granted through RBAC or access policies, not a key.",null,"A SAS is a storage or service token, not a Key Vault access mechanism, and would itself be a stored credential."]],["az5",0,1,"Five function apps need identical permissions to a storage account, and the identity must survive the apps being redeployed. Which identity type fits best?",["A system-assigned managed identity on each app","An app registration with a certificate credential","A user-assigned managed identity shared by the apps","A guest account invited into the tenant"],2,"A user-assigned managed identity is a standalone resource that can be attached to many apps and is not deleted with them. System-assigned identities are one per resource and deleted with it.","1: Managed identities",["System-assigned identities are one per resource and deleted with the app, so they neither survive redeploy as a shared identity nor share permissions across five apps.","An app registration with a certificate reintroduces a managed credential that must be stored and rotated.",null,"Guest accounts are for external human users, not app-to-service authentication."]],["az6",0,1,"Users are consenting to third-party apps that request access to their mailbox. The security team wants user consent limited to verified publishers and low-impact permissions, with an admin reviewing other requests. What should they configure?",["User consent settings plus the admin consent workflow","Redirect URI restrictions on each app registration","A Conditional Access policy blocking unknown locations","A PIM access review of the Global Administrators"],0,"User consent settings can restrict consent to verified publishers and selected permissions, and the admin consent workflow routes other requests to reviewers. Redirect URIs do not control consent.","1: App registrations and consent",[null,"Redirect URIs only control where tokens are returned to an app, not what permissions users may consent to.","Conditional Access governs sign-in conditions such as location, not application consent.","A PIM access review evaluates admin role membership, which is unrelated to third-party app consent."]],["az7",0,1,"What is created in a tenant when a multitenant application is first consented to there?",["A new app registration in the tenant","A user-assigned managed identity for the app","A guest user account for the publisher","A service principal (enterprise application)"],3,"Consent creates a service principal, shown as an enterprise application, representing the app in that tenant. The app registration exists only in the home tenant.","1: App registrations and consent",["The app registration exists only in the application's home tenant; consenting in another tenant does not create one there.","Consent does not create a managed identity for the application.","Consent does not create a guest user for the publisher.",null]],["az8",0,1,"A company wants to require MFA for all users signing in to the Azure portal, except when they are on the corporate network. What should be built?",["Per-user MFA enabled for every account in the tenant, with app passwords","A Conditional Access policy for Azure management that excludes a trusted named location","Security defaults with the corporate IP ranges added as trusted IPs","An Azure Policy assignment that audits sign-ins from outside the office"],1,"Conditional Access can target the Azure management app and exclude a named location marked trusted. Security defaults cannot be customized with exclusions, and Azure Policy does not govern sign-in.","1: Conditional Access",["Per-user MFA is legacy, cannot exclude the corporate network, and app passwords weaken security.",null,"Security defaults are all-or-nothing and cannot be customized with location exclusions.","Azure Policy governs resource configuration and cannot enforce or exclude sign-in behavior."]],["az9",0,1,"Before enforcing a new Conditional Access policy that blocks legacy authentication, an engineer wants to see which sign-ins it would affect without blocking anyone. What should they do?",["Assign it to an empty group","Set the policy to Report-only","Enable it and review the audit log","Create a PIM access review"],1,"Report-only mode evaluates the policy on every sign-in and logs the result without enforcing it. Enabling it would block users immediately, and an empty group shows no impact at all.","1: Conditional Access",["Assigning the policy to an empty group puts no one in scope, so it reveals nothing about the real impact.",null,"Enabling the policy enforces the block on legacy auth immediately, which is exactly what they want to avoid first.","A PIM access review evaluates role membership, not the impact of a Conditional Access policy."]],["az10",0,1,"Administrators must use phishing-resistant sign-in. Which Conditional Access grant control enforces this most directly?",["Require the device to be marked compliant in Intune","Require an approved client app on mobile platforms","Require a password change on every sign-in","Require an authentication strength of phishing-resistant MFA"],3,"Authentication strengths let a policy require specific methods, such as FIDO2 security keys or Windows Hello for Business. A compliant device does not stop an attacker from phishing the credentials themselves.","1: Authentication methods",["A compliant device proves device health but does nothing to stop an attacker who phishes the user's credentials.","Requiring an approved client app enforces app protection on mobile but is not phishing-resistant authentication.","Forcing a password change every sign-in churns passwords without preventing phishing; passwords remain phishable.",null]],["az11",0,1,"A Key Vault uses the access policy model. A user with Contributor on the vault has no access policy but can still read secrets after a few minutes. Why?",["Contributor can edit the vault's access policies and grant itself access","The Contributor role includes secret data actions by default","Soft delete exposes recently deleted secrets to all Contributors","Access policies are ignored for users who hold the Contributor role"],0,"Under access policies, anyone who can write the vault resource can edit its access policies. The RBAC permission model separates management from data access, so Contributor alone cannot read secrets.","1: Key Vault",[null,"Contributor is a control-plane role with no data actions, so it does not include secret read permissions by default.","Soft delete retains deleted objects for recovery; it does not grant anyone read access to live secrets.","Contributor is not exempt from access policies; it gains access only by editing those policies, not by bypassing them."]],["az12",0,1,"An auditor requires that deleted keys in a Key Vault cannot be permanently removed by anyone before the retention period ends. What must be enabled?",["Soft delete only","A ReadOnly resource lock","Purge protection","Key rotation policy"],2,"Purge protection prevents permanent deletion of a vault or its objects while they are in the soft-deleted state, even by administrators. Soft delete alone still allows a purge.","1: Key Vault",["Soft delete retains deleted objects for a recovery window but still permits an early purge.","A ReadOnly lock blocks resource writes and would break normal vault use; it does not stop purging soft-deleted objects.",null,"A key rotation policy schedules new key versions and has nothing to do with preventing permanent deletion."]],["az13",0,1,"A security engineer wants Azure to rotate a customer-managed key in Key Vault every 90 days automatically. What should be configured?",["A Logic App that deletes the old key","Purge protection on the vault","A key rotation policy on the key","An expiration date on the vault"],2,"Key Vault key rotation policies generate a new key version on a schedule and can notify before expiry. Services that use versionless key URIs pick up the new version automatically.","1: Key Vault",["A Logic App that deletes the old key is custom, does not generate a scheduled new version, and deleting an in-use key breaks services.","Purge protection prevents permanent deletion of soft-deleted objects; it does not rotate keys.",null,"A vault has no expiration that rotates keys; an expiry date on a key simply makes it unusable when reached."]],["az14",0,1,"New storage accounts must never allow public blob access. Existing accounts should be reported but not changed. Which Azure Policy effect meets the requirement for new resources?",["Deny","Audit","AuditIfNotExists","Disabled"],0,"Deny blocks create or update requests that violate the rule, while existing resources are only evaluated and marked non-compliant. Audit would allow new non-compliant accounts.","1: Azure Policy",[null,"Audit only flags non-compliant accounts after the fact and would still allow new accounts with public access.","AuditIfNotExists evaluates based on a related resource's existence and still does not block creation of new accounts.","Disabled turns the policy off entirely, enforcing nothing."]],["az15",0,1,"A policy must automatically add a diagnostic setting to every new Key Vault that is missing one. Which effect and prerequisite are needed?",["Modify, with no identity required on the assignment","Deny, with a CanNotDelete lock on each vault","Append, with a policy exemption for existing vaults","DeployIfNotExists, with a managed identity for remediation"],3,"DeployIfNotExists deploys a related resource when it is missing, and the assignment needs a managed identity with rights to deploy it. Modify changes properties of the resource itself, such as tags.","1: Azure Policy",["Modify changes properties on the evaluated resource (such as tags) and cannot deploy a separate diagnostic setting; remediation also requires an identity.","Deny would block creating the vault rather than adding the missing diagnostic setting.","Append adds fields to the request payload; it cannot deploy a diagnostic setting resource.",null]],["az16",0,1,"Twenty related policy definitions must be assigned together to many subscriptions and tracked as one compliance score. What should be created?",["A resource lock","A policy initiative","A management group per definition","A custom RBAC role"],1,"An initiative (policy set) groups definitions so they can be assigned and reported together. Management groups are scopes, not groupings of policies.","1: Azure Policy",["A resource lock prevents delete or modify operations; it does not group policy definitions.",null,"Management groups are assignment scopes, not a way to bundle definitions into one compliance score.","A custom RBAC role grants permissions and has nothing to do with grouping policies."]],["az17",0,1,"An Owner tries to delete a resource group and receives an error even though they have full permissions. What is the most likely cause?",["The Owner role lacks delete actions","The resource group has a CanNotDelete lock","A Conditional Access policy blocks deletions","PIM is enabled for the subscription"],1,"Locks apply to everyone, including Owners, until removed. Owner includes all actions, and Conditional Access evaluates sign-ins, not specific delete operations.","1: Governance",["Owner includes all actions, including delete, so a missing permission is not the cause.",null,"Conditional Access evaluates sign-ins, not individual resource delete operations.","PIM governs role activation and does not block an Owner from deleting a resource group."]],["az18",0,1,"A ReadOnly lock is placed on a storage account. What unexpected side effect is common?",["Blob data is re-encrypted with a new Microsoft-managed key","Users can no longer sign in to the Azure portal at all","Blob reads through Entra RBAC are blocked on the data plane","Listing the account keys fails because it is a POST operation"],3,"ReadOnly blocks all write and POST management operations, and listing keys is a POST, so tools that fetch keys break. Data-plane access through Entra RBAC is not blocked by the lock.","1: Governance",["A resource lock does not trigger re-encryption of blob data.","A resource lock does not affect a user's ability to sign in to the Azure portal.","ReadOnly restricts management-plane writes, not data-plane blob reads through Entra RBAC.",null]],["az19",0,1,"A team needs a role that can restart VMs and read their configuration but cannot create or delete them. No built-in role matches. What should be created?",["A custom Azure role with the needed actions","A Microsoft Entra administrative unit","A PIM approval workflow","An Azure Policy initiative"],0,"A custom role lists exactly the allowed actions, such as Microsoft.Compute/virtualMachines/read and restart/action, with assignable scopes. Administrative units scope Entra directory roles, not Azure resource actions.","1: Azure RBAC",[null,"An administrative unit scopes Entra directory roles, not Azure resource actions such as restarting a VM.","A PIM approval workflow governs activation of an existing role; it does not create a new permission set.","An Azure Policy initiative enforces compliance and does not grant restart permissions."]],["az20",0,1,"Which field in a custom Azure role definition grants permission to read blob contents rather than manage the storage account?",["actions","notActions","dataActions","assignableScopes"],2,"dataActions cover data-plane operations such as reading blobs or queue messages. actions cover control-plane operations on the resource itself.","1: Azure RBAC",["actions cover control-plane operations on the resource itself, not reading blob contents.","notActions subtracts control-plane permissions and does not grant data-plane access.",null,"assignableScopes defines where the role can be assigned, not what data it can access."]],["az21",0,1,"The CISO wants guest users with access to production resource groups reviewed by resource owners every quarter, with access removed if not approved. What should be used?",["Azure Policy compliance reports","Defender for Cloud secure score","Microsoft Entra access reviews","Resource locks on each group"],2,"Access reviews run recurring reviews of group memberships, app assignments or role assignments and can auto-remove denied access. Policy compliance does not review who has access.","1: Governance",["Azure Policy compliance reports show resource configuration compliance, not who has access to resource groups.","Secure score measures overall security posture and does not review guest access assignments.",null,"Resource locks prevent changes to resources; they do not review or remove user access."]],["az22",0,1,"Microsoft Entra ID Protection reports a user as high risk after leaked credentials were found. Which control forces a secure password change at next sign-in?",["A user risk Conditional Access policy requiring password change","A sign-in frequency session control set to one hour","A named location that marks the office network as trusted","A Key Vault access policy that removes the user's secrets"],0,"User risk reflects the likelihood an identity is compromised, and a risk-based policy can require a secure password change with MFA. Sign-in frequency only changes how often users reauthenticate.","1: Identity protection",[null,"Sign-in frequency only changes how often users reauthenticate; it does not force a secure password reset after a leak.","A named location marks a network as trusted and does nothing to remediate a high-risk user.","A Key Vault access policy controls vault permissions, not the user's account password."]],["az23",0,2,"A developer needs to give a partner time-limited read access to one blob container. The storage account has key access disabled. Which SAS type can still be used?",["Account SAS","Service SAS","Stored access policy SAS","User delegation SAS"],3,"A user delegation SAS is signed with a key obtained through Entra credentials, so it works when Shared Key is disabled. Account and service SAS are signed with the account key.","2: Storage",["An account SAS is signed with the account key, which is disabled here.","A service SAS is also signed with the account key and is unavailable when Shared Key is disabled.","A stored access policy still governs a service or account SAS signed with the account key, so it cannot be used either.",null]],["az24",0,2,"A service SAS was issued with a one-year expiry and must be revoked today without affecting other SAS tokens signed with the same key. What should have been done when it was issued?",["Sign it with the secondary account key","Link it to a stored access policy","Set the HTTPS-only protocol flag on it","Include a narrow allowed IP address range"],1,"A SAS tied to a stored access policy can be revoked by changing or deleting that policy. Rotating the signing key would invalidate every SAS signed with it.","2: Storage",["Signing with the secondary key does not allow revoking one token without invalidating every SAS signed with that key.",null,"The HTTPS-only flag enforces transport security but does not make a SAS revocable.","A narrow IP range limits where the SAS works but cannot revoke it before its expiry."]],["az25",0,2,"Compliance requires that financial records in Blob Storage cannot be modified or deleted for seven years, even by account owners. What should be configured?",["Soft delete for blobs with a 7-year retention","A locked time-based immutability (WORM) policy","A CanNotDelete resource lock on the account","Customer-managed keys with a rotation policy"],1,"A locked time-based retention policy makes blobs write-once, read-many for the retention period. A resource lock blocks deleting the account, not changing blob data.","2: Storage",["Soft delete retains deleted blobs for recovery but does not stop modification or deletion, which owners can still perform.",null,"A CanNotDelete lock blocks deleting the storage account, not modifying or deleting blob data within it.","Customer-managed keys control encryption, not blob immutability."]],["az26",0,2,"A regulator requires that the company control and be able to revoke the keys that encrypt a storage account. What should be enabled?",["Infrastructure encryption with Microsoft-managed keys","HTTPS-only secure transfer","A user delegation SAS","Customer-managed keys stored in Key Vault"],3,"Customer-managed keys let the organization own, rotate and revoke the key in Key Vault or Managed HSM. Infrastructure encryption adds a second layer but with Microsoft-managed keys.","2: Storage",["Infrastructure encryption adds a second encryption layer, but with Microsoft-managed keys the organization still cannot revoke them.","HTTPS-only protects data in transit and has nothing to do with encryption key ownership.","A user delegation SAS is an access token, not an encryption key you can control or revoke.",null]],["az27",0,2,"Uploaded files in a storage account should be checked for malware as they arrive, and the result written as a blob index tag. Which feature fits?",["Defender for Storage malware scanning","An Azure Policy DeployIfNotExists assignment","Blob soft delete with versioning turned on","Network Watcher packet capture on the subnet"],0,"Defender for Storage on-upload malware scanning scans new blobs and can tag results and raise alerts. The other options do not inspect file content.","2: Storage",[null,"A DeployIfNotExists policy remediates configuration; it does not inspect file content for malware.","Soft delete with versioning retains blob versions but does not scan uploads for malware.","Network Watcher packet capture records network traffic, not blob file content."]],["az28",0,2,"A storage account firewall allows only selected VNets. Azure Backup and Azure Monitor now fail to reach it. What is the simplest fix?",["Enable public network access from all networks instead","Rotate both account keys and update the services","Allow trusted Microsoft services to access the account","Disable secure transfer so the services can use HTTP"],2,"The trusted Microsoft services exception lets listed Azure services reach the account despite network rules. Opening all networks removes the protection entirely.","2: Storage",["Opening the account to all networks removes the firewall protection entirely.","Rotating the account keys does not grant those services a network path through the firewall.",null,"Disabling secure transfer allows HTTP and weakens security without fixing the network reachability."]],["az29",0,2,"An Azure SQL logical server must allow only Microsoft Entra identities to sign in, and SQL authentication must be refused. What should be enabled?",["Transparent data encryption","Dynamic data masking","Microsoft Entra-only authentication","Advanced Threat Protection email alerts"],2,"Entra-only authentication disables SQL logins for the server. TDE encrypts data at rest and does not affect sign-in.","2: Databases",["TDE encrypts data at rest and does not change which authentication methods are allowed.","Dynamic data masking obscures values in query results, not sign-in methods.",null,"Advanced Threat Protection email alerts notify on threats; they do not disable SQL authentication."]],["az30",0,2,"A payroll app stores national ID numbers in Azure SQL. DBAs must not be able to see plaintext values, even with sysadmin rights. What should be used?",["Always Encrypted","Dynamic data masking","Transparent data encryption","Row-level security"],0,"Always Encrypted encrypts columns on the client; keys never reach the database engine, so DBAs see only ciphertext. Dynamic data masking can be bypassed by privileged users and TDE decrypts data for any authorized query.","2: Databases",[null,"Dynamic data masking hides values in result sets, but users with UNMASK (such as DBAs) can still see plaintext.","TDE encrypts at rest but decrypts data for any authorized query, so sysadmins would see plaintext.","Row-level security filters which rows a user can see; it does not hide column values from privileged users."]],["az31",0,2,"Support staff should see only the last four digits of customer phone numbers in query results, while the app sees full values. No application changes are allowed. What fits?",["Always Encrypted","TDE with customer-managed keys","Server-level firewall rules","Dynamic data masking"],3,"Dynamic data masking hides data in result sets for users without UNMASK permission, with no app changes. Always Encrypted requires driver changes and hides the whole value.","2: Databases",["Always Encrypted hides the entire value and requires driver and application changes, which are not allowed here, and would block the app too.","TDE with customer-managed keys encrypts at rest and does not mask specific fields in results.","Server-level firewall rules control network access, not what data users see in results.",null]],["az32",0,2,"The security team wants to be alerted to SQL injection attempts and anomalous logins against Azure SQL databases. What should be enabled?",["SQL auditing to a storage account only","Microsoft Defender for SQL","Always Encrypted","Geo-replication"],1,"Defender for SQL provides vulnerability assessment and threat detection, including SQL injection and unusual access alerts. Auditing records events but does not analyze them for threats.","2: Databases",["Auditing to storage records events but does not analyze them for injection or anomalous logins.",null,"Always Encrypted protects column confidentiality; it does not detect attacks.","Geo-replication provides availability and disaster recovery, not threat detection."]],["az33",0,2,"An NSG has rule 200 denying TCP 3389 from Internet and rule 100 allowing TCP 3389 from 203.0.113.10. What happens to RDP from 203.0.113.10?",["It is denied, because deny rules always take precedence","It is allowed, because rule 100 is processed first","It is denied by the DenyAllInBound default rule","It depends on the route table attached to the subnet"],1,"NSG rules are processed in priority order from lowest number, and the first match decides. Deny rules do not automatically override allows.","2: Networking",["Deny rules do not automatically take precedence; NSGs evaluate by priority order.",null,"Rule 100 matches the traffic first at a lower priority number, so the DenyAllInBound default never applies.","The route table affects the traffic path, not the NSG allow-or-deny decision."]],["az34",0,2,"Web and database VMs share one subnet. The team wants NSG rules that say 'web servers may reach database servers on 1433' without listing IP addresses. What should be used?",["Service tags","User-defined routes","Private DNS zone records","Application security groups"],3,"Application security groups group VM NICs by role so NSG rules can reference them instead of IPs. Service tags represent Azure service address ranges, not your VMs.","2: Networking",["Service tags represent Microsoft's service address ranges, not your own virtual machines.","User-defined routes control traffic paths; they do not group VMs for NSG rules.","Private DNS zone records resolve names and do not group VMs for rule references.",null]],["az35",0,2,"An NSG outbound rule should allow VMs to reach Azure Storage in their region only, without tracking Microsoft's IP ranges. What should the destination be?",["The regional Storage service tag","An application security group for storage","The VirtualNetwork service tag","A static list of Azure public IP prefixes"],0,"Regional service tags such as Storage.WestEurope are maintained by Microsoft and update automatically. The VirtualNetwork tag covers your address spaces, not Azure services.","2: Networking",[null,"An application security group groups your VM NICs, not Azure service endpoints like Storage.","The VirtualNetwork service tag covers your own VNet address spaces, not the Azure Storage service.","A static list of prefixes is brittle and requires manual updates as Microsoft changes its ranges."]],["az36",0,2,"The central network team must block inbound SSH from the internet across 200 VNets, and application teams must not be able to override it with their own NSGs. What should be used?",["An NSG on every subnet that app teams manage themselves","Service endpoints enabled on every subnet in each VNet","Azure Virtual Network Manager security admin rules","Azure DDoS Network Protection on each virtual network"],2,"Security admin rules in Virtual Network Manager are evaluated before NSGs and can deny traffic that NSGs cannot re-allow. NSGs owned by app teams could be changed by those teams.","2: Networking",["An NSG managed by app teams could be changed or overridden by those teams, defeating the central mandate.","Service endpoints extend VNet identity to PaaS services; they do not block inbound SSH.",null,"DDoS Network Protection mitigates volumetric attacks and does not block a specific port."]],["az37",0,2,"An Azure SQL database must be reachable only through a private IP from the VNet and from on-premises over ExpressRoute, with public access disabled. What should be implemented?",["A service endpoint for Microsoft.Sql on the subnet","A server firewall rule for the VNet address range","A private endpoint with a private DNS zone","An application security group for the database tier"],2,"A private endpoint gives the database a private IP reachable from the VNet, peered VNets and on-premises. Service endpoints only work from the enabled subnet and keep the public endpoint.","2: Networking",["A service endpoint only works from the enabled subnet and keeps the public endpoint reachable.","A server firewall rule for the VNet range still uses the public endpoint and does not provide a private path over ExpressRoute.",null,"An application security group groups VM NICs; it does not give the PaaS database a private IP."]],["az38",0,2,"After creating a private endpoint for a storage account, VMs still resolve the account's name to its public IP. What is most likely missing?",["A privatelink private DNS zone linked to the VNet","An NSG rule allowing outbound port 443 to Storage","Customer-managed keys configured on the account","A user-defined route sending 0.0.0.0/0 to the internet"],0,"Name resolution must return the private IP, which is done by the privatelink private DNS zone linked to the VNet (or equivalent custom DNS). NSG rules do not change DNS answers.","2: Networking",[null,"An NSG rule for port 443 permits traffic but does not change DNS resolution to return the private IP.","Customer-managed keys concern encryption and are unrelated to name resolution.","A user-defined route to the internet would push traffic away from the private endpoint, not fix resolution."]],["az39",0,2,"Outbound HTTPS traffic from workloads must be decrypted and checked against intrusion signatures. Which service meets this?",["Azure Firewall Standard","NSG flow logs","Application Gateway Basic","Azure Firewall Premium"],3,"Azure Firewall Premium adds TLS inspection and signature-based IDPS. Standard offers threat intelligence filtering but does not decrypt TLS.","2: Networking",["Azure Firewall Standard offers threat intelligence filtering but does not decrypt TLS or provide full IDPS.","NSG flow logs record flow metadata; they cannot inspect decrypted payloads.","Application Gateway Basic is a layer-7 load balancer and does not provide TLS inspection with IDPS.",null]],["az40",0,2,"All internet-bound traffic from a spoke subnet must pass through an Azure Firewall in the hub. What must be configured on the spoke subnet?",["An NSG on the spoke subnet that denies all outbound internet traffic","A route table with 0.0.0.0/0 pointing to the firewall's private IP","A service endpoint for Microsoft.Web on the spoke subnet","A private DNS zone for the firewall linked to the spoke VNet"],1,"A user-defined route for 0.0.0.0/0 with the firewall's private IP as the virtual appliance next hop forces traffic through it. An NSG deny would block the traffic instead of redirecting it.","2: Networking",["An NSG outbound deny would block the traffic rather than redirect it through the firewall.",null,"A service endpoint for Microsoft.Web extends VNet identity to a PaaS service; it does not route internet-bound traffic.","A private DNS zone for the firewall resolves names and does not force the traffic path."]],["az41",0,2,"A public web app behind Application Gateway suffers SQL injection and cross-site scripting attempts. What should be enabled on the gateway?",["DDoS Network Protection on the gateway's VNet","Web Application Firewall with a managed rule set","NSG flow logs on the gateway subnet","A private endpoint for the backend app"],1,"WAF on Application Gateway inspects HTTP requests using managed OWASP-based rules to block injection and XSS. DDoS Protection handles volumetric attacks, not application-layer payloads.","2: Networking",["DDoS Network Protection mitigates volumetric network floods, not application-layer injection or XSS payloads.",null,"NSG flow logs record traffic metadata and cannot block malicious HTTP requests.","A private endpoint for the backend restricts network access but does not inspect requests for injection attacks."]],["az42",0,2,"A company wants to publish a global web app with edge caching and layer 7 protection close to users worldwide. Which combination fits?",["Application Gateway with a public IP in one region","Azure Firewall Standard in each spoke","VPN Gateway with forced tunneling","Azure Front Door with a WAF policy"],3,"Front Door is a global edge service and its WAF policy filters traffic at the edge. Application Gateway is regional.","2: Networking",["Application Gateway is a regional service, so a single-region deployment has no global edge caching close to users worldwide.","Azure Firewall Standard per spoke is a network firewall, not a global edge web delivery, caching or WAF service.","A VPN Gateway with forced tunneling provides private connectivity, not public global web delivery.",null]],["az43",0,2,"Remote workers need access to specific private apps in Azure without a full network-level VPN, based on identity and Conditional Access. What fits?",["Microsoft Entra Private Access","Point-to-site VPN with certificate auth","A site-to-site VPN","Azure Bastion"],0,"Entra Private Access is a zero trust network access service that publishes private apps per identity with Conditional Access. A point-to-site VPN gives network-level access to the VNet.","2: Networking",[null,"A point-to-site VPN grants network-level access to the VNet rather than per-app, identity-based access.","A site-to-site VPN connects entire networks and offers no per-app Conditional Access.","Azure Bastion brokers RDP and SSH to VMs; it is not a general private-app access service."]],["az44",0,2,"A VM cannot reach a database on port 1433. The engineer wants to know which NSG rule is dropping the packet. Which tool answers this fastest?",["Azure Advisor","Defender for Cloud secure score","Network Watcher IP flow verify","Azure Monitor autoscale"],2,"IP flow verify tests a specific 5-tuple against the effective NSG rules and names the rule that allows or denies it. Advisor gives general recommendations.","2: Networking",["Azure Advisor gives general best-practice recommendations, not per-packet NSG rule evaluation.","Secure score measures posture and does not test a specific 5-tuple against NSG rules.",null,"Autoscale scales compute resources and is unrelated to diagnosing NSG rules."]],["az45",0,2,"The security team wants a record of accepted and denied traffic across a VNet, with visual analysis of top talkers. What should be enabled?",["Boot diagnostics on every virtual machine","Connection troubleshoot on each VM","VNet flow logs with traffic analytics","Azure Monitor metrics alerts on each NIC"],2,"VNet flow logs record allowed and denied flows to storage and traffic analytics processes them in Log Analytics. Connection troubleshoot tests one connection at a time.","2: Networking",["Boot diagnostics capture a VM's boot output, not network flows.","Connection troubleshoot tests one connection at a time and offers no top-talker analysis.",null,"Metric alerts on NICs fire on thresholds but do not record accepted and denied flows."]],["az46",0,2,"A company has 30 branch offices and several Azure regions and wants centrally managed connectivity with Azure Firewall inspection in each hub. What fits best?",["Virtual WAN with secured virtual hubs","One hub VNet peered directly to every branch office","A point-to-site VPN client for each branch user","Service endpoints on every subnet in every region"],0,"Virtual WAN provides managed hubs for branch VPN, ExpressRoute and user VPN, and a secured hub adds Azure Firewall managed through Firewall Manager. Branches cannot be peered as VNets.","2: Networking",[null,"Branch offices are physical sites, not VNets, so they cannot be peered to a hub VNet.","A point-to-site client VPN per branch is per-user and does not provide managed site connectivity with per-hub firewalls.","Service endpoints extend a VNet to PaaS services and do not provide branch connectivity."]],["az47",0,2,"Public IP addresses in front of critical workloads need adaptive tuning, attack telemetry and cost protection against volumetric attacks. What should be enabled?",["Azure Firewall Basic SKU","Front Door WAF custom rules","NSG default inbound deny rules","Azure DDoS Protection"],3,"Azure DDoS Protection adds adaptive tuning, attack metrics and alerts, and cost protection beyond the default infrastructure protection. WAF addresses application-layer attacks.","2: Networking",["Azure Firewall Basic is a firewall SKU, not a volumetric DDoS mitigation with adaptive tuning and cost protection.","Front Door WAF custom rules filter application-layer traffic, not volumetric network attacks.","NSG default deny rules are static and provide no adaptive tuning or attack telemetry.",null]],["az48",0,3,"Management ports on VMs must be closed by default and opened only for a requested source IP for a few hours. What should be enabled?",["Azure Bastion Developer","Just-in-time VM access","An NSG allowing 3389 from any","Encryption at host"],1,"JIT, part of Defender for Servers Plan 2, keeps ports closed and adds temporary allow rules on approved requests. Bastion removes the need for public ports but does not open them on request.","3: Servers and VMs",["Azure Bastion Developer removes the need for public management ports but does not open them per request for a specific source IP.",null,"An NSG allowing 3389 from any leaves the port open to everyone, the opposite of closed-by-default.","Encryption at host encrypts disks and is unrelated to controlling port access."]],["az49",0,3,"Admins need RDP and SSH to VMs from the Azure portal without assigning public IPs to the VMs. What should be deployed?",["A NAT gateway","Azure Bastion","Application Gateway","A public load balancer"],1,"Bastion brokers RDP/SSH over TLS from the portal to the VM's private IP. A NAT gateway only handles outbound traffic.","3: Servers and VMs",["A NAT gateway handles outbound traffic only and cannot broker inbound RDP or SSH.",null,"Application Gateway is a layer-7 web load balancer, not an RDP/SSH broker.","A public load balancer distributes traffic and would still require public IPs, not private brokered access."]],["az50",0,3,"A new Windows VM must be protected against bootkits and rootkits with measured boot and a virtual TPM. Which option should be selected at creation?",["Azure Disk Encryption with BitLocker keys","Encryption at host with platform-managed keys","A dedicated host isolated for the company","Trusted launch (secure boot and vTPM)"],3,"Trusted launch provides secure boot, vTPM and boot integrity monitoring. Disk encryption protects data at rest, not the boot chain.","3: Servers and VMs",["Azure Disk Encryption protects data at rest, not the boot chain against bootkits and rootkits.","Encryption at host encrypts disks and caches but provides no secure boot or vTPM.","A dedicated host isolates the physical hardware but does not provide measured boot or a vTPM.",null]],["az51",0,3,"VM temporary disks and disk caches must be encrypted, and no agent may run inside the guest OS. What should be enabled?",["Encryption at host","Azure Disk Encryption","Always Encrypted","Storage Service Encryption only"],0,"Encryption at host encrypts temp disks and caches at the host level without an in-guest agent. Azure Disk Encryption uses BitLocker or DM-Crypt inside the guest.","3: Servers and VMs",[null,"Azure Disk Encryption uses an in-guest agent (BitLocker or DM-Crypt), which the requirement explicitly forbids.","Always Encrypted is a SQL column-protection feature, not VM disk encryption.","Storage Service Encryption covers the managed disk storage, not temp disks and host caches, and is not the named agentless feature."]],["az52",0,3,"On-premises and AWS servers should appear in Defender for Cloud and receive Defender for Servers protection alongside Azure VMs. What should be deployed on those servers?",["An Azure Bastion host in a peered VNet","A site-to-site VPN Gateway to each location","The Azure Connected Machine agent (Azure Arc)","The legacy Log Analytics (MMA) agent"],2,"Azure Arc projects servers outside Azure as Azure resources so Defender for Servers and Azure Policy can apply. The legacy Log Analytics agent is retired. AWS servers can also be onboarded through the AWS connector, which uses Arc.","3: Servers and VMs",["A Bastion host brokers RDP and SSH; it does not onboard servers into Defender for Cloud.","A site-to-site VPN provides network connectivity, not projection and management of servers as Azure resources.",null,"The legacy Log Analytics (MMA) agent is retired and does not project servers as Arc-enabled resources."]],["az53",0,3,"Which Defender for Servers plan includes just-in-time VM access and file integrity monitoring?",["Plan 1","Foundational CSPM","Plan 2","Defender for Storage"],2,"Plan 2 includes JIT, file integrity monitoring, agentless scanning and more. Plan 1 focuses on Defender for Endpoint integration.","3: Servers and VMs",["Plan 1 focuses on Defender for Endpoint integration and does not include JIT or file integrity monitoring.","Foundational CSPM is free posture management and does not include workload protection features like JIT or FIM.",null,"Defender for Storage protects storage accounts, not virtual machines."]],["az54",0,3,"The team wants to find missing OS updates on Azure and Arc-enabled servers and schedule patching windows. Which service fits?",["Azure Update Manager","Azure Advisor recommendations","Network Watcher connection monitor","Microsoft Purview Audit"],0,"Azure Update Manager assesses and installs updates on Azure and Arc-enabled machines with maintenance schedules. Advisor does not deploy patches.","3: Servers and VMs",[null,"Azure Advisor recommends best practices but does not assess or install OS updates.","Network Watcher connection monitor tests network connectivity, not patching.","Microsoft Purview Audit logs activity and does not manage updates."]],["az55",0,3,"Developers use kubectl with a shared admin kubeconfig for an AKS cluster. Security wants per-user sign-in with Entra ID and access governed by Azure roles. What should be enabled?",["Kubernetes network policies on each namespace","A private cluster with no public API endpoint","Defender for Containers runtime protection","Entra ID integration with Azure RBAC for Kubernetes"],3,"Entra integration authenticates users with Entra ID, and Azure RBAC for Kubernetes authorizes them through Azure role assignments; local accounts can then be disabled. Network policies control pod traffic, not user access.","3: App platforms",["Kubernetes network policies control pod-to-pod traffic, not user authentication to the cluster.","A private cluster hides the API endpoint but does not provide per-user Entra sign-in.","Defender for Containers provides threat protection, not user authentication or authorization.",null]],["az56",0,3,"The AKS API server must not be reachable from the internet at all. What should be configured?",["Authorized IP ranges only","A private cluster","A Kubernetes network policy","An Azure Policy add-on"],1,"A private cluster gives the API server a private IP only reachable from the VNet. Authorized IP ranges still expose a public endpoint, just filtered.","3: App platforms",["Authorized IP ranges still expose a filtered public API endpoint rather than making it fully private.",null,"A Kubernetes network policy controls pod traffic, not exposure of the API server.","An Azure Policy add-on enforces cluster governance and does not make the API server private."]],["az57",0,3,"Pods in the 'frontend' namespace must be allowed to reach 'backend' pods, and all other pod-to-pod traffic to backend must be blocked. What should be used?",["An NSG on the node pool subnet","Kubernetes network policies","Azure Firewall DNAT rules for the pods","Azure Bastion connected to the cluster"],1,"Network policies (Azure, Calico or Cilium) filter traffic between pods using labels and namespaces. NSGs work on node or subnet IPs and cannot see pod labels.","3: App platforms",["An NSG on the node subnet works on node and subnet IPs and cannot see pod labels or namespaces.",null,"Azure Firewall DNAT rules handle inbound NAT, not pod-to-pod filtering by label.","Azure Bastion is an RDP/SSH broker and is unrelated to pod traffic filtering."]],["az58",0,3,"A security team wants vulnerability assessment of container images, runtime threat detection on AKS nodes, and Kubernetes posture recommendations. Which plan provides this?",["Defender for App Service","Defender for Key Vault","Defender for Storage","Defender for Containers"],3,"Defender for Containers covers image vulnerability assessment, run-time protection and Kubernetes hardening for AKS and other clusters. The other plans cover different resource types.","3: App platforms",["Defender for App Service protects web apps, not containers or AKS clusters.","Defender for Key Vault protects vault access, not container images.","Defender for Storage protects storage accounts, not clusters.",null]],["az59",0,3,"AKS pulls images from Azure Container Registry using the registry's admin user password stored as a Kubernetes secret. What is the recommended replacement?",["Give the kubelet identity AcrPull and disable the admin user","Keep the admin user but rotate its password every month","Store the admin password in a ConfigMap instead of a secret","Make the registry public so no credentials are needed"],0,"Attaching ACR with the kubelet managed identity and AcrPull gives least-privilege, secretless pulls. Rotating a shared admin password keeps a shared credential with push rights.","3: App platforms",[null,"Keeping the admin user and rotating its password monthly still relies on a shared credential that includes push rights, a higher risk than least-privilege pull.","A ConfigMap is not meant for secrets and still exposes the shared admin credential.","Making the registry public exposes all images to anyone, a serious data exposure."]],["az60",0,3,"An App Service app stores a database connection string in plain text in app settings. The team wants it kept in Key Vault without code changes. What should they use?",["A deployment slot setting marked sticky to production","An Azure Policy Modify effect that encrypts app settings","A Key Vault reference using the app's managed identity","Easy Auth with Microsoft Entra ID for the web app"],2,"A Key Vault reference (@Microsoft.KeyVault(...)) resolves the secret at runtime using the app's managed identity, with no code change. Slot settings only control swap behavior.","3: App platforms",["A sticky slot setting controls swap behavior between deployment slots; it does not move the secret into Key Vault.","An Azure Policy Modify effect changes properties such as tags; it does not fetch secrets from Key Vault.",null,"Easy Auth authenticates end users to the app and does not store the connection string in Key Vault."]],["az61",0,3,"An internal App Service app should accept traffic only from the company's Application Gateway subnet. What should be configured?",["HTTPS only enabled on the web app","A minimum inbound TLS version of 1.2","Access restrictions allowing that subnet","A custom domain with a managed certificate"],2,"Access restrictions filter inbound requests by IP, service tag or VNet subnet. HTTPS only and TLS version protect traffic in transit but do not restrict sources.","3: App platforms",["HTTPS only encrypts traffic in transit and does not limit which sources can reach the app.","A minimum TLS version sets the protocol floor and does not filter inbound sources.",null,"A custom domain with a managed certificate handles naming and TLS, not source restriction."]],["az62",0,3,"A company wants users to sign in to an App Service web app with Microsoft Entra ID without writing authentication code. What should be enabled?",["App Service built-in authentication (Easy Auth)","A private endpoint for the web app's inbound traffic","Access restrictions that allow only the corporate IPs","A system-assigned managed identity on the web app"],0,"Built-in authentication handles sign-in with Entra ID and other providers before requests reach the code. A managed identity lets the app call other services, not users sign in.","3: App platforms",[null,"A private endpoint restricts the inbound network path; it does not provide user sign-in.","Access restrictions filter source IPs but do not authenticate users.","A system-assigned managed identity lets the app call other services, not sign users in."]],["az63",0,3,"An HTTP-triggered Azure Function uses a function key that has been shared widely. Which change best reduces risk while keeping callers working after an update?",["Change the authorization level to anonymous","Move the function key into the URL query string","Enable remote debugging to trace the callers","Require Entra ID authentication and use tokens"],3,"Using Entra authentication gives identity-based, revocable access instead of a shared key. Anonymous removes protection entirely.","3: App platforms",["Setting the authorization level to anonymous removes protection entirely.","Putting the key in the URL query string keeps a shared key and makes it more likely to be logged or leaked.","Remote debugging is a diagnostic feature and does nothing to secure access.",null]],["az64",0,3,"A Logic App must call an Azure Resource Manager API. The team wants no stored credentials in the workflow. What should they use?",["A client secret in a parameter","A managed identity on the Logic App","A storage account key","A SAS token in the trigger URL"],1,"Logic Apps can authenticate outbound calls with a managed identity that holds the needed Azure role. A client secret is still a stored credential.","3: App platforms",["A client secret in a parameter is still a stored credential that must be managed and rotated.",null,"A storage account key is a stored credential and is the wrong mechanism for calling the ARM API.","A SAS token in the trigger URL is a stored token, not identity-based authentication for ARM."]],["az65",0,3,"A generative AI chatbot built on Microsoft Foundry is being hit with prompts that try to override its system instructions. The security team wants alerts in Defender for Cloud. What should be enabled?",["Defender for Storage on the training data","Defender for AI services threat protection","Just-in-time VM access for the app servers","Azure DDoS Protection on the public endpoint"],1,"Defender for AI services raises alerts for threats such as jailbreak and prompt injection attempts against AI workloads, using Prompt Shields signals. DDoS Protection handles network floods.","3: Security for AI",["Defender for Storage protects storage accounts, not runtime prompt-injection attempts against an AI app.",null,"Just-in-time VM access controls VM management ports and is unrelated to prompt attacks.","DDoS Protection mitigates network floods, not prompt injection."]],["az66",0,3,"Several apps call the same Azure OpenAI deployment with API keys, and one app consumes most of the tokens. The team wants central authentication and per-app token limits. What should be deployed?",["A private endpoint for each calling app","A separate Azure OpenAI resource per app","An NSG in front of the OpenAI resource","An AI Gateway in API Management"],3,"An API Management AI gateway can authenticate to the backend with a managed identity and apply token limit policies per consumer. Private endpoints restrict network paths but do not enforce quotas.","3: Security for AI",["A private endpoint per app restricts the network path but does not enforce per-app token quotas.","A separate Azure OpenAI resource per app multiplies resources without central authentication or quota control.","An NSG filters network traffic and cannot meter tokens or centrally authenticate callers.",null]],["az67",0,3,"Before rolling out Microsoft 365 Copilot, the company wants to find sensitive files that are overshared and could surface in Copilot answers. Which tool fits?",["Microsoft Purview DSPM for AI","Azure Firewall Premium TLS inspection","Defender for Servers file integrity monitoring","Network Watcher traffic analytics"],0,"Purview Data Security Posture Management for AI reports oversharing and sensitive data exposure risks for Copilot and other AI apps. The other tools do not analyze data exposure in Microsoft 365.","3: Security for AI",[null,"Azure Firewall Premium TLS inspection examines network traffic, not oversharing of Microsoft 365 data.","Defender for Servers file integrity monitoring watches file changes on servers, not Copilot data exposure.","Network Watcher traffic analytics analyzes network flows, not data exposure to AI apps."]],["az68",0,3,"AI agents in the tenant act with their own identities. The security team wants to block agents from signing in when they are flagged as risky. Which feature supports this?",["A resource lock on the agent","An NSG on the agent subnet","Conditional Access for Microsoft Entra Agent ID","Azure Policy Audit effect"],2,"Microsoft Entra Agent ID gives agents identities that Conditional Access policies can target, including risk-based blocking. Resource locks and NSGs do not control identity sign-in.","3: Security for AI",["A resource lock prevents changes to a resource; it does not control identity sign-in.","An NSG on the agent subnet filters network traffic, not identity risk at sign-in.",null,"An Azure Policy Audit effect flags configuration compliance; it does not block a risky agent from signing in."]],["az69",0,3,"A Foundry model should reject inputs that contain hate speech and should flag outputs that include protected material. What should be configured?",["A WAF custom rule on the Front Door profile","Key Vault purge protection on the model's vault","Content filters and guardrails on the deployment","Transparent data encryption on the chat history"],2,"Foundry deployments use content filters and guardrails (Azure AI Content Safety) to screen prompts and completions. A WAF inspects HTTP patterns, not model content categories.","3: Security for AI",["A WAF custom rule on Front Door inspects HTTP patterns, not model content categories such as hate speech.","Key Vault purge protection protects vault objects and is unrelated to content moderation.",null,"TDE on chat history encrypts stored data; it does not screen inputs or outputs for content."]],["az70",0,4,"The security team wants to see chains such as 'internet-exposed VM with a critical vulnerability and a managed identity that can read sensitive storage'. Which Defender for Cloud feature shows this?",["Attack path analysis in Defender CSPM","The regulatory compliance dashboard view","Workflow automation with a Logic App","Secure score history in the overview blade"],0,"Attack path analysis, part of the paid Defender CSPM plan, maps exploitable paths to high-value assets. The compliance dashboard only maps controls to standards.","4: Defender for Cloud",[null,"The regulatory compliance dashboard maps controls to standards; it does not chart exploitable attack chains.","Workflow automation runs Logic Apps in response to events; it does not visualize attack paths.","Secure score history trends the score over time and does not map exploit chains to assets."]],["az71",0,4,"Which capability is included in free foundational CSPM?",["Attack path analysis","Agentless vulnerability scanning","Cloud security explorer queries","Secure score and security recommendations"],3,"Foundational CSPM includes secure score, recommendations, asset inventory and the Microsoft cloud security benchmark. The others require Defender CSPM.","4: Defender for Cloud",["Attack path analysis is a paid Defender CSPM feature, not part of free foundational CSPM.","Agentless vulnerability scanning requires Defender CSPM or workload plans, not free CSPM.","Cloud security explorer is part of the paid Defender CSPM plan.",null]],["az72",0,4,"An engineer fixes a recommendation on 8 of 10 storage accounts. How does secure score change for that control?",["It gains the full control score immediately","It gains points in proportion to the healthy resources","It does not change until the next monthly recalculation","It drops, because partial remediation is penalized"],1,"Secure score awards control points in proportion to healthy resources, and the full points only when every resource is healthy. Scores refresh within hours, not monthly.","4: Defender for Cloud",["The full control score is awarded only when every resource is healthy, not after fixing 8 of 10.",null,"Secure score refreshes within hours, not on a monthly recalculation.","Partial remediation raises the score proportionally; it is not penalized with a drop."]],["az73",0,4,"An auditor wants to see how the Azure environment maps to PCI DSS controls. Where should the engineer add this?",["Import a PCI template from the Sentinel workbooks gallery","Add the standard in regulatory compliance","Filter the Azure Advisor recommendations by PCI tag","Run an Entra ID access review scoped to cardholder apps"],1,"The regulatory compliance dashboard assesses resources against the Microsoft cloud security benchmark and extra standards such as PCI DSS that you add. Workbooks visualize data but do not assess compliance.","4: Defender for Cloud",["Workbooks visualize data and do not assess resources against a compliance standard like PCI DSS.",null,"Azure Advisor gives recommendations and has no PCI compliance mapping to filter by.","An Entra access review evaluates who has access; it does not map resources to PCI controls."]],["az74",0,4,"Recommendations keep being ignored. The CISO wants each one assigned to a resource owner with a due date and email reminders. What should be configured?",["A Sentinel watchlist","An Azure Policy exemption","A resource lock","Governance rules in Defender for Cloud"],3,"Governance rules assign owners and due dates to recommendations automatically and send reminders. An exemption would hide the recommendation instead of fixing it.","4: Defender for Cloud",["A Sentinel watchlist is a reference list for KQL, not a way to assign recommendation owners or reminders.","An Azure Policy exemption hides the recommendation rather than driving a fix.","A resource lock prevents resource changes and does not assign owners or due dates.",null]],["az75",0,4,"The company also runs workloads in AWS and wants their posture in the same Defender for Cloud secure score. What should be created?",["An AWS connector in Defender for Cloud","A site-to-site VPN between Azure and AWS","A Sentinel watchlist that lists the AWS account IDs","A management group named AWS with the accounts in it"],0,"Multicloud connectors onboard AWS accounts and GCP projects for posture management and workload protection. A VPN only provides network connectivity.","4: Defender for Cloud",[null,"A site-to-site VPN provides network connectivity, not posture onboarding into secure score.","A Sentinel watchlist of account IDs is a reference list and does not bring AWS posture into Defender for Cloud.","A management group organizes Azure subscriptions, not AWS accounts."]],["az76",0,4,"Every high-severity Defender for Cloud alert should automatically trigger a Logic App that opens a ticket. What should be configured?",["A Sentinel hunting query","An Azure Policy Audit effect","Workflow automation in Defender for Cloud","A Key Vault rotation policy"],2,"Workflow automation runs a Logic App when alerts, recommendations or compliance changes match your conditions. Hunting queries are run manually.","4: Defender for Cloud",["A Sentinel hunting query is run manually and does not auto-trigger on Defender for Cloud alerts.","An Azure Policy Audit effect flags configuration compliance and does not run a Logic App on alerts.",null,"A Key Vault rotation policy rotates keys and is unrelated to alert automation."]],["az77",0,4,"A Linux appliance sends CEF-formatted logs. How should they be collected into Microsoft Sentinel today?",["The legacy Log Analytics agent installed directly on the appliance","A daily script that uploads the log files to a storage account","A Linux forwarder running the Azure Monitor Agent with a CEF via AMA rule","The appliance configured to send syslog to the Sentinel portal URL"],2,"The supported method uses the Azure Monitor Agent on a forwarder and a data collection rule through the CEF via AMA connector. The legacy Log Analytics agent is retired.","4: Sentinel",["The legacy Log Analytics agent installed on the appliance is retired and is not the supported CEF ingestion path.","A daily script uploading files to storage is not a supported real-time CEF ingestion method for Sentinel.",null,"Sentinel has no direct portal syslog endpoint; CEF requires a forwarder running the Azure Monitor Agent."]],["az78",0,4,"An analyst wants a Sentinel rule that detects role assignment creation within about a minute of it happening. Which rule type is designed for this?",["Near-real-time (NRT) analytics rule","Scheduled rule running every 24 hours","Anomaly rule","Hunting bookmark"],0,"NRT rules run every minute over the latest data for fast detection. A daily scheduled rule would be too slow, and bookmarks are not detections.","4: Sentinel",[null,"A rule scheduled every 24 hours is far too slow for near-real-time detection within about a minute.","An anomaly rule detects statistical deviations and is not designed for ~1-minute event detection.","A hunting bookmark saves query results and is not an automated detection."]],["az79",0,4,"Sentinel incidents from a noisy test subscription should automatically be closed as benign, and all others assigned to the tier 1 queue. What should be used?",["A playbook for each incident","A workbook","A watchlist","Automation rules"],3,"Automation rules handle simple triage such as changing status, assigning owners and adding tags without a Logic App. Playbooks are for external or multi-step actions.","4: Sentinel",["A playbook is a Logic App for external or multi-step actions, which is overkill for simple status changes and assignment.","A workbook visualizes data and does not triage incidents.","A watchlist is a reference list and does not close or assign incidents.",null]],["az80",0,4,"When a Sentinel incident involves a compromised user, the SOC wants to disable the account in Entra ID and post in a Teams channel automatically. What should be built?",["An analytics rule with entity mapping enabled","A playbook triggered by an automation rule","A saved hunting query with a livestream","A data collection rule with a transformation"],1,"Playbooks carry out external actions such as disabling users or posting messages, and an automation rule can trigger them on incident creation. Entity mapping only identifies entities.","4: Sentinel",["An analytics rule with entity mapping identifies entities but does not disable users or post messages.",null,"A hunting query with a livestream monitors for hits; it does not take external actions.","A data collection rule with a transformation shapes ingested data and cannot disable users or post to Teams."]],["az81",0,4,"Analytics rule results should show the affected user and host so incidents can be correlated and investigated in the graph. What should be configured in the rule?",["Alert grouping disabled","Entity mapping","A shorter lookback","Suppression"],1,"Entity mapping tells Sentinel which query columns are accounts, hosts, IPs and so on, enabling correlation and investigation. Suppression stops the rule from running after a match.","4: Sentinel",["Disabling alert grouping changes how alerts bundle into incidents; it does not label entity columns.",null,"A shorter lookback changes the query time window, not entity correlation.","Suppression stops the rule from running after a match and does not map entities."]],["az82",0,4,"The SOC maintains a list of VIP accounts that analytics rules should treat as high priority. Where should the list live so KQL can reference it?",["A Key Vault secret","An Azure Policy parameter","A resource tag","A Sentinel watchlist"],3,"Watchlists are uploaded lists that KQL can join with the _GetWatchlist() function. Key Vault and policy parameters are not queryable from Sentinel rules.","4: Sentinel",["A Key Vault secret is not queryable from a KQL analytics rule.","An Azure Policy parameter governs policy evaluation and is not referenceable by Sentinel KQL.","A resource tag is metadata on a resource and is not joinable within analytics rules.",null]],["az83",0,4,"Firewall logs must be kept for two years for investigations, but are rarely queried and are too costly in the Analytics tier. What should the engineer consider?",["A lower-cost long-term tier such as the Sentinel data lake","Deleting the logs after 30 days to stay within budget","Copying each day's logs into a Sentinel watchlist","Disabling the firewall connector until an incident occurs"],0,"Lower-cost tiers keep high-volume, rarely queried data for long periods at reduced cost while still allowing queries. Deleting logs breaks the retention requirement.","4: Sentinel",[null,"Deleting the logs after 30 days breaks the two-year retention requirement.","A watchlist is a small reference list, not a mechanism for bulk long-term log retention.","Disabling the connector loses the logs entirely, defeating the retention requirement."]],["az84",0,4,"A threat hunter wants to look proactively for signs of an attacker technique that has no alert yet, and save interesting results for later. What should be used?",["Workflow automation in Defender for Cloud","Automation rules that close incidents","Hunting queries and bookmarks","Secure score recommendations"],2,"Hunting queries search data for indicators, and bookmarks save results for investigation or promotion to incidents. Automation rules act on incidents that already exist.","4: Sentinel",["Workflow automation acts on Defender for Cloud alerts and is not for proactive hunting of undetected techniques.","Automation rules act on existing incidents, not on techniques that have raised no alert yet.",null,"Secure score recommendations provide posture guidance, not proactive threat hunting."]],["az85",0,4,"Which KQL query returns failed sign-ins from the last hour grouped by user?",["SigninLogs | where TimeGenerated > ago(1h) and ResultType == \"0\" | summarize count() by UserPrincipalName","AuditLogs | where TimeGenerated > ago(1h) | summarize count() by OperationName, Identity","SigninLogs | where TimeGenerated > ago(1h) and ResultType != \"0\" | summarize count() by UserPrincipalName","SecurityEvent | where TimeGenerated > ago(1h) and EventID == 4624 | summarize count() by Account"],2,"ResultType 0 means success, so != \"0\" returns failures, and summarize groups by user. The other queries return successful sign-ins or unrelated data.","4: Sentinel",["ResultType == \"0\" returns successful sign-ins, not failed ones.","AuditLogs summarized by OperationName queries directory audit events, not failed sign-ins.",null,"SecurityEvent EventID 4624 is a successful Windows logon, not an Entra failed sign-in."]],["az86",0,4,"Users report that Microsoft Security Copilot responses are slow and prompts are being throttled during a busy incident. What is the most likely fix?",["Provision more SCUs or allow overage","Assign all analysts the Security Reader role","Enable more Sentinel data connectors","Disable promptbooks for the whole tenant"],0,"Security Copilot capacity is measured in SCUs, and throttling means capacity is exhausted. Adding roles or connectors does not add capacity.","4: Security Copilot",[null,"Assigning the Security Reader role grants read permissions and does not add compute capacity.","Enabling more Sentinel connectors adds data sources, not Copilot capacity.","Disabling promptbooks removes a feature and does not relieve capacity-based throttling."]],["az87",0,4,"An analyst wants Security Copilot to run the same series of prompts for every phishing investigation. What should they create?",["A watchlist","A workbook","A data collection rule","A promptbook"],3,"Promptbooks are saved sequences of prompts that can be run together with inputs. Workbooks visualize data and do not run Copilot prompts.","4: Security Copilot",["A watchlist is a Sentinel reference list and cannot run Copilot prompts.","A workbook visualizes data and does not sequence and run prompts.","A data collection rule shapes log ingestion and is unrelated to Copilot prompts.",null]],["az88",0,4,"Security Copilot needs to answer questions using data from a third-party threat intelligence service. What should be added?",["A new SCU region","A plugin for that service","A Sentinel automation rule","A Purview sensitivity label"],1,"Plugins extend Security Copilot with data and skills from Microsoft and third-party sources. SCUs add capacity, not new data sources.","4: Security Copilot",["A new SCU region adds capacity, not new data sources.",null,"A Sentinel automation rule automates incident handling and does not extend Copilot's data.","A Purview sensitivity label classifies data and does not connect a threat intelligence source to Copilot."]],["az89",0,4,"During an investigation, the team needs to know who deleted a SharePoint file and when. Which tool records this activity?",["Defender for Cloud secure score","Microsoft Purview Audit","Network Watcher flow logs","Azure Update Manager"],1,"Purview Audit (unified audit log) records user and admin activity across Microsoft 365, including file deletions. Flow logs only record network traffic.","4: Operations",["Secure score measures security posture and does not record who deleted a specific file.",null,"Network Watcher flow logs record network traffic, not file activity in Microsoft 365.","Azure Update Manager manages patching and does not record audit activity."]],["az90",0,4,"A company wants Microsoft Defender XDR incidents, including those from Defender for Cloud, to flow into Sentinel without creating duplicate incidents. What should be used?",["A separate scheduled analytics rule for each Defender alert","A daily CSV export of alerts imported into a custom table","A watchlist of alert IDs joined to the SecurityAlert table","The Defender XDR connector with incident integration"],3,"The Defender XDR connector syncs incidents and alerts bidirectionally, avoiding duplicates. Separate rules per alert would create duplicate incidents.","4: Sentinel",["A separate scheduled analytics rule per Defender alert would create duplicate incidents.","A daily CSV export into a custom table is manual, delayed, and does not sync incidents without duplicates.","A watchlist of alert IDs joined to SecurityAlert is a reference list, not a bidirectional incident sync.",null]]]);
